(window.GROUP_QUIZZES=window.GROUP_QUIZZES||{})["clinical-medicine-and-surgery-i-exam-4-coronary-artery-disease-vignettes"]={"title":"Coronary Artery Disease Vignettes 1 — CMS I Exam 4","category":"Clinical Medicine and Surgery I","exam":"Exam 4","sem":"fall-2026","questions":[{"q":"A 69-year-old man has 40 minutes of chest pressure. His 12-lead electrocardiogram shows ST depression in V1, V2 and V3 with no ST elevation in any lead. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Order a coronary calcium score","Record posterior leads V7-V9","Repeat the tracing next week","Treat as stable angina"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. ST depression in V1-V3 can be the reciprocal view of a posterior infarct. Posterior leads V7-V9 show the ST elevation directly, confirming a posterior infarct from the right coronary or circumflex artery. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 62"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has episodes of jaw and tooth pain when she walks uphill that ease when she rests. Her dentist found no dental cause. Which is the most likely cause of her pain?","choices":["Gallbladder disease","Pancreatitis","Aortic dissection","Myocardial ischemia"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Exertional pain relieved by rest is ischemic, and acute coronary syndrome refers pain most commonly to the jaw, tooth or arm. Toothache is even listed among the descriptors of angina. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 26, 31"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction is pain-free and stable on aspirin, clopidogrel and a beta blocker. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["A thrombolytic infusion","Discharge, no further testing","Coronary calcium scoring","Left heart catheterization"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Following initial stabilization, unstable angina and non-ST-elevation infarction proceed to left heart catheterization with coronary angiography to find the lesion, then angioplasty or a stent as needed. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 50"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with atypical chest discomfort has a normal electrocardiogram and two high-sensitivity troponin I values of 3 and 4 ng/L. She asks what the blood test showed. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["It proves her arteries are clear","It rules in a heart attack","It cannot be used in women","It safely rules out a heart attack"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. High-sensitivity troponin is used mainly to rule out cardiac injury and send people home safely; it is less useful as a positive predictor. Two high-sensitivity troponin I values of 3 and 4 ng/L sit well below the women's 12 ng/L. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 56-year-old woman with an ST-elevation myocardial infarction takes a phosphodiesterase inhibitor for pulmonary arterial hypertension; her last dose was six hours ago. Which medication is contraindicated?","choices":["Nitroglycerin","Chewable aspirin","Clopidogrel","A heparin drip"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Nitroglycerin is contraindicated within 24 hours of a phosphodiesterase inhibitor. These drugs are prescribed for pulmonary arterial hypertension as well as erectile dysfunction, so everyone is asked. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 73"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman is admitted with a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction. Her low-density lipoprotein cholesterol is 68 mg/dL, and she has never taken a statin. What is the most appropriate statin plan?","choices":["A statin only if her cholesterol rises","High-intensity statin before discharge","No statin, since her cholesterol is low","Moderate-intensity statin after discharge"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Every patient with an acute coronary syndrome gets high-intensity statin therapy regardless of baseline low-density lipoprotein cholesterol, and it should be started before hospital discharge. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 81"},{"q":"A 45-year-old woman with untreated hyperthyroidism has chest pressure whenever she climbs stairs. She asks how her thyroid condition could cause chest pain. Which explanation is most accurate?","choices":["It raises the heart's oxygen demand","It lowers the blood's oxygen supply","It narrows the coronary arteries","It triggers coronary artery spasm"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Myocardial ischemia is a mismatch between oxygen demand and supply. Hyperthyroidism sits on the increased-demand side, with exercise, emotional stress, left ventricular hypertrophy, hyperthermia and cocaine. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 5, 6"},{"q":"A 68-year-old man is recovering from a large anterior wall myocardial infarction and is already on aspirin. Which additional agent is recommended for his infarct?","choices":["Warfarin","Streptokinase","Eptifibatide","Amlodipine"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is recommended after infarction, and warfarin is additionally recommended in patients with large anterior wall myocardial infarctions, which is his situation. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 83"},{"q":"A 35-year-old woman has confirmed vasospastic angina with early-morning episodes at rest. She has stopped smoking and carries sublingual nitroglycerin for symptoms. Which is the first-line preventive therapy?","choices":["Nonselective beta blocker","Dual antiplatelet therapy","Coronary stent placement","Calcium channel blocker"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. First-line therapy for vasospastic angina is a calcium channel blocker such as diltiazem or amlodipine, alongside stopping smoking and nitrates for symptoms. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man had a drug-eluting stent placed two weeks ago for an acute coronary syndrome. He tells you the clopidogrel is expensive and he plans to stop it because his chest pain is gone. What should he be told?","choices":["It can stop once the chest pain is gone","Missed doses matter only if pain returns","Stopping now risks sudden restenosis","Aspirin alone now protects the stent"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Noncompliance with dual antiplatelet therapy (a P2Y12 receptor antagonist such as clopidogrel plus aspirin) in the first 6 weeks after a stent carries about a 50% risk of sudden in-stent restenosis. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 52"},{"q":"A 57-year-old man has substernal pressure whenever he mows the lawn. He asks whether changing his position will ease the pain if it is angina. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Leaning forward usually relieves it","Deep breathing usually relieves it","Angina is not affected by position","Lying flat usually relieves it"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Anginal pain is not affected by position and typically worsens with exertion, because exertion raises myocardial oxygen demand. Rest and nitroglycerin relieve it. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 27"},{"q":"A 60-year-old woman has chest pressure only with brisk walking, relieved by rest. Lifestyle changes, a statin, aspirin and sublingual nitroglycerin are started. Which daily medication class is added first?","choices":["Beta blocker","Calcium channel blocker","Thrombolytic agent","Intravenous nitrate"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Stable angina treatment is lifestyle modification, a beta blocker, a statin and aspirin, with sublingual nitroglycerin for attacks. A calcium channel blocker is used only if beta blockers are contraindicated. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 39"},{"q":"A 55-year-old man who received a heart transplant has exertional shortness of breath and fatigue but no chest pain. His daughter asks how he could have heart disease without pain. Which explanation is accurate?","choices":["Chest pain appears only with a full infarct","His shortness of breath rules out the heart","Transplanted hearts can have silent ischemia","Transplanted hearts cannot become ischemic"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A transplanted heart is one of the settings for silent ischemia, along with diabetes and peripheral neuropathy: chest discomfort may be absent, and shortness of breath or fatigue may be the only signs. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 29, 30"},{"q":"A 47-year-old woman with chest pressure in the emergency department asks whether a coronary calcium score could show whether she is having a heart attack. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Yes; a score of zero excludes one","Yes, if done within the hour","No; it estimates future risk","Yes; a high score confirms one"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. A cardiac computed tomography calcium score is a risk stratification tool, not a test for acute coronary syndrome. A higher score means higher future risk, not an infarct in progress. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 35"},{"q":"A 57-year-old woman with an ST-elevation myocardial infarction arrives at a hospital with a catheterization lab. What is the goal time from her arrival to balloon inflation?","choices":["Under 90 minutes","Under 30 minutes","Under 10 minutes","Under 3 hours"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. The goal door-to-balloon time is under 90 minutes. Time is muscle: the electrocardiogram within 10 minutes starts the clock, and the catheterization team is called at once. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 68"},{"q":"A 66-year-old man with severe multivessel coronary disease is scheduled for a coronary artery bypass graft. Which conduit is the first choice to bypass his obstructions?","choices":["The popliteal vein","A mammary or radial artery","The great saphenous vein","The superficial femoral vein"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Bypass grafting anastomoses one or both of the mammary arteries, or a radial artery, to the coronary artery distal to the obstructive lesion. A vein is used only if an artery cannot be used. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 76, 77"},{"q":"A 52-year-old woman and her 52-year-old husband ask at a wellness visit whether their age already counts as a coronary risk factor. Which answer is accurate?","choices":["Only for her","For both of them","For neither of them","Only for her husband"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. Age becomes a nonmodifiable coronary risk factor at 45 or older for men and 55 or older for women. At 52 he has crossed his threshold and she has not. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 22"},{"q":"A 56-year-old man with chest discomfort has a first high-sensitivity troponin I of 16 ng/L. Which is the most appropriate next step?","choices":["Discharge on this one value","Start thrombolytic therapy","Call it abnormal and admit","Draw a second troponin I"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. For a man the high-sensitivity troponin I 99th percentile is 20 ng/L, so 16 ng/L is not abnormal, but one value never stands alone: when you get one, you get two, and watch the change. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 61-year-old man has new chest pain at rest, T-wave inversion on his electrocardiogram and a standard troponin I of 0.01 ng/mL. What does this troponin I result indicate?","choices":["It needs no repeat draw","It is not abnormal","It confirms an infarction","It is abnormal for a man"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. A standard troponin I is abnormal above 0.04 ng/mL, so 0.01 ng/mL is not abnormal. Rest pain with nonspecific T-wave changes and a normal troponin is unstable angina, and the troponin I is still repeated. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 46, 47; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 40-year-old man with vasospastic angina, now on a calcium channel blocker, read that daily aspirin prevents heart attacks and asks whether he should start it. What should he be told?","choices":["Aspirin is avoided in this condition","Aspirin prevents future spasm attacks","Take aspirin only during an episode","Add aspirin once he quits smoking"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Aspirin is on the avoid list for vasospastic angina, together with nonselective beta blockers and triptans. His prevention is a calcium channel blocker plus stopping smoking. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 92"},{"q":"A 60-year-old man with an occluded coronary artery is being rushed to the catheterization laboratory. His wife asks how quickly heart muscle is permanently damaged. Which time frame is most accurate?","choices":["About 24 to 48 hours","About 40 to 60 minutes","About 5 to 10 minutes","About 6 to 8 hours"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. The heart is only 0.3% of body weight but consumes 7% of total oxygen demand, and irreversible injury occurs in about 40 to 60 minutes of ischemia. That is why rapid reperfusion matters. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 7"},{"q":"A 58-year-old woman has chest pain with ST elevation in V1 through V4. Which pattern of ischemia does ST elevation reflect?","choices":["Microvascular dysfunction","Transmural ischemia","Subendocardial ischemia","Reversible demand ischemia"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Transmural ischemia, involving the full thickness of the wall, produces ST elevation in the leads over that region. Subendocardial ischemia of the inner wall produces ST depression instead. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 55"},{"q":"A 55-year-old woman has microvascular angina confirmed after angiography showed normal coronary arteries. Which treatment is appropriate?","choices":["Reassurance alone","Calcium channel blocker","Coronary stent placement","Thrombolytic therapy"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Treatment of microvascular angina is very similar to coronary artery disease: lifestyle modification and risk-factor reduction, a calcium channel blocker, beta blocker, angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor and statin. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 96"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman is being discharged after a drug-eluting stent was placed for a non-ST-elevation myocardial infarction. She is on aspirin and clopidogrel. Which other drug class belongs in her discharge regimen?","choices":["Calcium channel blocker","Fibrinolytic infusion","High-intensity statin","Glycoprotein 2b/3a inhibitor"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Medical optimization after stent placement is dual antiplatelet therapy, a statin and an angiotensin-converting enzyme inhibitor, with anticoagulation if indicated. Every acute coronary syndrome patient gets a high-intensity statin before discharge. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 52, 81"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman has an hour of substernal pressure. Her electrocardiogram shows ST-segment depression of 2 mm in V5 and V6 without ST elevation, and her standard troponin I is 0.62 ng/mL. Which is the most likely diagnosis?","choices":["Unstable angina","Infarction with ST elevation","Vasospastic angina","Infarction without ST elevation"],"answer":3,"exp":"Correct. ST-segment depression above 1 mm and/or T-wave inversion in two or more consecutive leads, with symptoms and an elevated troponin I (standard troponin I above 0.04 ng/mL), is a non-ST-elevation infarction. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 47, 86; Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026 - SUPPLEMENT slide 72a"},{"q":"A 68-year-old woman with right ventricular infarction (ST elevation in V4R) is given sublingual nitroglycerin in triage, and her blood pressure falls to 70/40 mmHg. What explains the fall?","choices":["Ventricular aneurysm","Lost preload","Myocardial rupture","Thrombolytic bleeding"],"answer":1,"exp":"Correct. Nitroglycerin reduces preload, which removes the Frank-Starling effect the injured right ventricle depends on. Output drops further, the right ventricle fails and the patient goes into shock. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slide 63"},{"q":"A 44-year-old woman with vasospastic angina also has migraines. Her medication list includes amlodipine, diltiazem, sublingual nitroglycerin and sumatriptan. Which medication should she avoid?","choices":["Sumatriptan","Amlodipine","Diltiazem","Sublingual nitroglycerin"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Triptans such as sumatriptan can precipitate coronary vasospasm and are on the avoid list for vasospastic angina, along with nonselective beta blockers and aspirin. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 89, 92"},{"q":"A 62-year-old man has crushing chest pain. His electrocardiogram shows ST elevation in leads II, III and aVF. Which artery is most likely occluded?","choices":["Diagonal branch artery","Left anterior descending","Right coronary artery","Left marginal branch"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Leads II, III and aVF are the inferior leads, and inferior ST elevation points to the right coronary artery (and/or the circumflex). The right coronary artery also supplies the atrioventricular node. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 61, 62"},{"q":"A 36-year-old man who smokes a pack a day has vasospastic angina with episodes at rest in the early morning. He takes amlodipine and asks which lifestyle change matters most. What should he be told?","choices":["Follow a low-salt diet","Sleep sitting upright","Stop smoking","Avoid all exertion"],"answer":2,"exp":"Correct. Cigarette smoking is a major risk factor for vasospastic angina, and stopping smoking is the first line of its treatment, alongside a calcium channel blocker and nitrates for symptoms. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 89, 92"},{"q":"A 62-year-old woman with exertional chest tightness develops T-wave inversion during an exercise stress test. Which test is the gold standard for confirming coronary artery disease?","choices":["Coronary angiography","Nuclear stress test","Transthoracic echocardiogram","Coronary calcium score"],"answer":0,"exp":"Correct. Coronary angiography, with dye injected at catheterization and pictures taken of the coronary arteries, is the gold standard for diagnosing coronary artery disease after a positive stress test. — Coronary Artery Disease Carter 2026.pptx, Slides 35, 38"}]};
